# estige relay node — Oracle Linux package env file.
#
# Read by systemd's EnvironmentFile= for estige-relay.service. Mode 0600
# root:root.
#
# NOTHING SECRET LIVES HERE (d236). The relay's identity and its one pairing
# line live in the store under $XDG_DATA_HOME/estige (this unit sets
# /var/lib/estige/.local/share, a StateDirectory it owns):
#
#   relay.json = {"endpoint":"https://vpn.example.test/vpn",
#                 "exit":[{"name":"exit-home","public":"<cert>"}]}
#
# The endpoint IS the pairing's dial fact (d132) and the `exit` rows are the
# certificates it expects; `estige key check` validates the file before boot.
#
# Optional deployment label carried in the HELLO and the session records.
# VPN_CLIENT_LABEL=
#
# argv, not env: a cleartext `ws://` endpoint needs `-insecure-exit` in the
# unit (a drop-in changes ExecStart), and `-L [<bind>:]<port>:<host>:<port>`
# adds a local forward (docs/d144/d171). Both are visible in the process's
# own command line on purpose.
#
# The relay's own upstream chain (docs/d094), if it must egress through
# another proxy. Leave unset for a direct dial. These samples deliberately
# name another host: a value naming THIS relay's listeners is refused as a
# self-chain (exit 94). Conventional names:
# SOCKS_SERVER=proxy.example.test:1080
# SOCKS_VERSION=5
# HTTP_PROXY=http://proxy.example.test:3128
# HTTPS_PROXY=http://proxy.example.test:3128
# ALL_PROXY=http://proxy.example.test:3128
